course: everyday
lesson: 445
title: The older kid's «trade»
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A Year 8 kid in Manchester offered Ollie, in Year 5, a trade: «My shiny card for your three good ones, it's a steal for you, our kid.» The shiny card was a fake printed at home. Ollie's brother saw it and made the older kid give the cards back. Ollie was embarrassed but relieved. Older kids sometimes use their size and confidence on younger ones: a deal that looks generous, said fast, by someone bigger than you, with no time to check.
explain: At the park in Brooklyn, a teen told Jaylen, «Lemme borrow your scooter real quick, I'll bring it right back, no cap» — «no cap» is slang for «no lie». He rode off round the corner and didn't come back. Jaylen's dad found the scooter dumped two streets away, scratched. In Liverpool a big lad told Ben, «Swap you my phone for your bike, it's boss, worth way more» — in Liverpool «boss» means brilliant. The phone was locked to someone else's account. Ben's mum asked where it came from; it went to the police, and Ben got his bike back — just. A swap «worth way more» usually hides something: broken, stolen or fake.
explain: Some «deals» are just taking. An older girl at Isla's school in Edinburgh said «You can be in our group if you give us your lunch money every Friday». Isla paid for three weeks before her mum noticed and the school stopped it. Real friends don't charge. Callum's cousin, 14, took his birthday money to «buy you a game online, my card's faster». The game never came: «Ach, it glitched, the money's gone.» Money doesn't vanish — the cousin had spent it, and paid it back week by week, mardy (sulky) with Callum for a month.
explain: Watch for invented prices and fuzzy words. At the skatepark in Adelaide a teen told Kai, «Mate, you owe me twenty bucks for that lesson.» He'd shown Kai one trick, for fun; nobody had said a price. Kai's dad had a word, and suddenly it was «a joke». Sam did his big brother's chores for a week for «my old PS4». His brother said «Psych! I meant my PS4 games case» («psych» means «tricked you»). After Mum made it fair, Sam always said deals back out loud: «So I get exactly what?»
explain: Small cons are everywhere. At a school fair an older boy sold Mia «magic wishing stones» — gravel from the car park — for a pound each. A teen on a bus in Singapore offered Rohan «a better phone case, half price, can or not?» («can or not» means «yes or no?»). It cracked the next day, and Mum said «Cheap-cheap sometimes costs more, lah.» Aisha's big neighbour «looked after» her tablet and downloaded games with her mum's card. The wise moves: ask someone you trust before a «great deal», keep your things with you, and say «Nah, sorry» — it is a full answer.
fact: Older kids sometimes use their size and confidence to get unfair trades from younger ones.
fact: A trade that seems far too generous often hides something broken, stolen or fake.
fact: Asking someone you trust before a «great deal» stops most tricks.
fact: «I'll bring it right back» from someone you don't know is a common way things get stolen.
fact: Friendship you have to pay for is not friendship; it is taking, and a grown-up should know.
fact: Money spent online does not vanish; it goes somewhere and leaves a receipt.
fact: You do not owe money for something when no price was agreed beforehand.
fact: Saying a deal back out loud, exactly, stops tricks with fuzzy words.
fact: Nobody can sell real magic; if they could, they would not sell it for a pound.
fact: Things sold by strangers on buses or in car parks usually cannot be returned.
fact: Keep your own things with you; «looking after» something does not mean using it.
fact: «Nah, sorry» is a full answer; you don't have to give reasons to a pushy older kid.
fact: «Boss» (Liverpool) means brilliant; «our kid» (Manchester) means brother or mate; «psych» (US) means «tricked you»; «can or not» and «lah» are Singapore English; a «quid» is a pound (UK money).
exercise: What was wrong with the shiny card the Year 8 kid offered Ollie? | It was a fake printed at home | It was the rarest card in the set | It was a card from a different game | It was bent right in the middle
exercise: Who helped Ollie get his cards back? | His brother, who saw the trade | The Year 8 kid's teacher | A police officer in the playground | Nobody, he lost them forever
exercise: What did the teen at the Brooklyn park do with Jaylen's scooter? | Rode off and didn't bring it back | Gave it back a week later with a new wheel | Asked Jaylen's dad first | Bought it for twenty dollars
exercise: What does «no cap» from a stranger prove? | Nothing at all | That he is honest | That he is a friend | That he is older
exercise: Why was the phone the big lad offered Ben worthless to Ben? | It was locked to someone else's account | It was made of cheap plastic and cracked | It had no games or apps on it at all | It was the wrong colour
exercise: What did the older girls really want from Isla? | Her lunch money every Friday | To be her real friends | Help with their homework every night | Her new school shoes
exercise: What did Callum's cousin say happened to the birthday money? | «It glitched, the money's gone» | «The game is coming next year» | «Your mum took the money back» | «I gave it to charity»
exercise: Where had Callum's money really gone? | The cousin spent it on himself | It disappeared inside the internet | The game company kept it by mistake | It went back to Callum's bank
exercise: Did Kai owe the teen twenty dollars for the trick? | No, nobody agreed a price | Yes, every lesson costs money | Yes, but only ten dollars | Only if he learned the trick
exercise: How did Sam's brother trick him? | With a fuzzy word — «PS4» meant only a case | He gave Sam a broken PS4 | He gave Sam a PS5 instead | He did half of the chores himself, so it was a fair swap
exercise: What did Sam start doing before deals? | Saying exactly what he'd get, out loud | Doing all the chores first, before agreeing anything | Never doing any chores at all | Asking for twice as much
exercise: What were the «magic wishing stones» really? | Gravel from the car park | Crystals from a deep mine in Wales | Stones from the beach | Glass from a shop
exercise: What did Rohan's mum mean by «cheap-cheap sometimes costs more»? | A poor cheap thing can cost you more in the end | Cheap things are always the best, whatever they're made of | Buy everything twice to be safe | Phone cases are always expensive
exercise: What did Aisha's neighbour do while «looking after» her tablet? | Downloaded games with her mum's card | Cleaned the screen for her | Charged it for her | Kept it safe in a drawer while she was at school
exercise: What is a full answer to a pushy older kid's «deal»? | «Nah, sorry.» | «Okay, if you promise.» | «Let me think for a year.» | «Only if you're nice.»
exercise: What does «boss» mean in Liverpool? | Brilliant | Angry and cross | Very expensive | Very old
test: Year 9 lad: «swap ya my phone for ur bike, it's boss, worth way more, quick». Best move? | Ask why — and say no or ask a grown-up | Swap fast before he changes his mind about the bike | Swap but keep the bike's helmet | Ask him to swear on his life
test: Older girl: «be in our crew, just bring us 2 quid every Friday». What is this really? | Taking, not friendship — tell a grown-up | A normal way to make friends | A good deal for being popular with the big girls | A school club with a fee
test: Teen at the park: «lemme hold your scooter real quick, no cap». What does a smart kid say? | Nah, sorry | Okay, but only round the corner | Sure, if you say no cap again | Take my helmet as well
test: Cousin: «Ach, it glitched, money's gone.» What's the best question? | Can I see the receipt? | Which glitch was it, exactly? | Can you glitch it back? | Is the internet broken?
test: «Mate, you owe me twenty bucks for that lesson.» No price was ever said. What's true? | You don't owe it — tell a grown-up | You owe it because he's older and he says so | You owe half of it | You must pay by Friday
test: Big brother: «Do my chores, I'll give you my PS4.» What would stop the trick? | Saying back «the whole console, yes?» | Doing the chores twice as fast | Doing the chores at night | Asking Dad to do the chores
test: What would happen if Ollie had asked his brother before trading? | His brother would have spotted the fake | His brother would have taken the cards for himself | The trade would be even better | Nothing would have changed
test: Older boy at the fair: «magic stones, make ur wishes come true, a quid each». What's true? | Nobody sells real magic for a pound | They work if you wish hard enough | Only the blue ones work | They work for younger kids only
test: Teen on the bus in Singapore: «half price case, can or not?» What is he asking? | Yes or no — will you buy it? | Can you carry it for me? | Do you have a case already? | Is the bus late?
test: In a cartoon, a little kid out-trades a big bully and wins a fortune. What's more common in real life? | The older kid gets the better deal | Little kids always win trades | Trades with older kids are always fair | Older kids always lose
test: Ben's mum: «where did this phone come from, love?» Why does she ask? | It may be stolen — that matters a lot | She wants a phone herself | She loves the colour of it | She wants to swap it for a car
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a trade proposed by an older child as being of much greater value should arouse suspicion. | The article offered may be counterfeit, defective or stolen | Older children are prohibited from trading | Valuable articles are never traded | Younger children always gain by such trades, older children being generous
exam: It is given that no price was agreed prior to the provision of an informal demonstration. Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a subsequent demand for payment. | No sum is owed, the demand being invented afterwards | The full sum demanded is owed | Half of the sum demanded is owed | The sum is owed if demanded by an older person
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises membership of a group conditional upon weekly payment? | It constitutes taking rather than friendship and ought to be reported | It constitutes a customary condition of friendship among older pupils at school | It constitutes a fair exchange of services | It constitutes a school regulation
exam: Determine which of the following measures most effectively prevents deception through imprecise wording. | Stating aloud the exact terms of the agreement before accepting it | Completing one's obligations with maximum speed, before the agreement can be altered | Accepting any agreement offered by a relative | Declining to ask questions of the other party
